{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's adversarial-perturbation design cannot satisfy its own success criterion. It states perturbations should 'modify inputs to satisfy checklist items without altering the ground-truth outcome.' But the problem asks for tasks 'with objectively verifiable outcomes' where the checklist is supposed to be a causal proxy for those outcomes. If the perturbation must leave the objective outcome unchanged while changing features the checklist uses as causal proxies, then either (a) the checklist items are genuinely causal for the outcome, in which case perturbing them necessarily changes the outcome — making the perturbation impossible — or (b) the checklist items are non-causal, in which case 'satisfying checklist items' without changing the outcome is trivially achievable by swapping one non-causal surface feature for another, but that only tests whether the panel rigidly keys on that specific surface feature; it does not distinguish a checklist that causally tracks the right answer from one that merely complies with a different but equally non-causal surface proxy. SRC_001's countercondition (perfect causal completeness) concedes the impossibility for the genuine-gain case, undermining the test's applicability exactly when the target claim most needs to demonstrate genuine gain.","counterexample":[{"expert_behavior":"Robust if intuition uses causal structure; fooled if it uses the same surface correlate — yet SRC_001 offers no independent reason the expert would differ.","input":"Medical diagnosis task: checklist item = 'fever present'; outcome = bacterial infection (ground truth via lab test). Perturbation: replace fever with a different non-causal correlate of infection, e.g., elevate inflammatory marker reading while removing fever, keeping infection status unchanged.","panel_behavior":"Checklist is 'satisfied' by the inflammatory marker if the item is phrased generally, or fails it if phrased narrowly as 'fever' — but either way the perturbation only probes surface-feature rigidity, not whether the checklist causally tracks infection."}],"target":"068151b8c14821d2d1d6b36b62c9df0b89d7f067762b18fb02233a0df199bfbc"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 restricts its entire argument to 'continuous outcome space and high-dimensional feature space' and rests the 'against' case on information loss from dimensional reduction. But the problem's claim is explicitly scoped to 'tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes,' which spans both continuous/high-dimensional tasks AND discrete/low-dimensional tasks (e.g., binary classification, rule-governed verification). SRC_002 concedes via its counterconditions that the argument collapses when 'the task outcome is determined by a small number of strong features' or when the checklist is exhaustive. For the large class of objectively-verifiable tasks that are discrete or low-dimensional — precisely where checklists are most commonly deployed and most plausibly reliable — SRC_002 provides no argument against the panel, and its evidence criterion (systematic underconfidence on outliers) is undefined or trivial when there are no meaningful 'outliers' in a narrow, discrete outcome space. Thus SRC_002 fails to argue against the claim as stated for the full scope the problem specifies.","counterexample":[{"panel_prediction":"Panel applies checklist, outcome is verifiable, low-dimensional; SRC_002's lossy-compression and outlier-underconfidence objections do not apply.","src_002_prediction_gap":"SRC_002's mechanism ('checklist forces discarding weak features in high-dimensional continuous space') predicts nothing here; its evidence criterion ('underconfidence on outliers') has no outlier distribution to detect.","task":"Binary compliance audit: outcome = 'passes regulatory check' (verifiable yes/no); checklist has 10 binary items; feature space is effectively the 10 checklist dimensions."}],"target":"a82f11763f38c1b29b214d3a7d2dd58621b436e34ad428a261fbb4957ccfe502"}]}